1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,289
More than $80 billion.

2
00:00:01,602 --> 00:00:03,808
That's what Anthropic raised in the biggest IPO

3
00:00:03,843 --> 00:00:04,470
in history.

4
00:00:04,899 --> 00:00:05,968
So is it a boom,

5
00:00:06,327 --> 00:00:07,221
or a bust?

6
00:00:08,127 --> 00:00:09,497
Days before the listing,

7
00:00:09,578 --> 00:00:11,169
AI's best-known skeptic,

8
00:00:11,238 --> 00:00:14,129
Gary Marcus, said the company might never turn

9
00:00:14,164 --> 00:00:14,861
a profit.

10
00:00:15,348 --> 00:00:16,591
Then, on the same show,

11
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:19,214
Box CEO Aaron Levie made the case for

12
00:00:19,261 --> 00:00:19,435
it.

13
00:00:19,923 --> 00:00:22,059
This is two hours of Big Technology in

14
00:00:22,105 --> 00:00:22,883
eight minutes:

15
00:00:23,243 --> 00:00:24,218
the bear case,

16
00:00:24,532 --> 00:00:25,495
the bull case,

17
00:00:25,855 --> 00:00:27,272
what actually happened,

18
00:00:27,504 --> 00:00:29,513
and the one number both of them agree

19
00:00:29,582 --> 00:00:30,650
will decide it.

20
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:31,559
Part one.

21
00:00:31,989 --> 00:00:32,952
The bear case.

22
00:00:33,382 --> 00:00:34,601
First, a surprise.

23
00:00:35,089 --> 00:00:38,246
Gary Marcus, the scientist who spent years saying

24
00:00:38,328 --> 00:00:40,278
chatbots alone would hit a wall,

25
00:00:40,545 --> 00:00:42,577
actually praises Anthropic.

26
00:00:43,065 --> 00:00:44,609
He says its coding tool,

27
00:00:44,876 --> 00:00:48,660
Claude Code, mixes language models with classic rule-based

28
00:00:48,684 --> 00:00:49,473
software,

29
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:52,445
which is exactly what he argued AI needed.

30
00:00:52,805 --> 00:00:54,756
So the product isn't his problem.

31
00:00:55,116 --> 00:00:56,021
The price is.

32
00:00:56,590 --> 00:00:59,400
Going in, the IPO was expected to value

33
00:00:59,446 --> 00:01:02,198
Anthropic at up to $1.4 trillion.

34
00:01:02,975 --> 00:01:05,018
Marcus wrote that the company has never made

35
00:01:05,042 --> 00:01:05,692
a profit,

36
00:01:06,052 --> 00:01:08,269
except for one quarter he calls anomalous and

37
00:01:08,339 --> 00:01:09,465
heavily subsidized.

38
00:01:09,825 --> 00:01:12,902
And it carries nearly $900 billion in future

39
00:01:12,936 --> 00:01:13,610
commitments.

40
00:01:14,039 --> 00:01:16,280
His analogy: you can make the world's best

41
00:01:16,361 --> 00:01:18,579
lemonade, but if you sell it for less

42
00:01:18,602 --> 00:01:19,867
than the lemons cost,

43
00:01:19,914 --> 00:01:21,191
it's still a bad business.

44
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:23,211
His bottom line is blunt.

45
00:01:23,571 --> 00:01:25,846
He wouldn't be shocked to see Anthropic go

46
00:01:25,916 --> 00:01:26,845
out of business.

47
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:29,619
His most likely outcome?

48
00:01:29,747 --> 00:01:30,885
It gets sold,

49
00:01:31,082 --> 00:01:33,253
for a lot less than a trillion dollars.

50
00:01:33,741 --> 00:01:36,028
If you want the AI money story explained

51
00:01:36,051 --> 00:01:37,050
like this every week,

52
00:01:37,131 --> 00:01:39,650
subscribe now, so you don't miss the next

53
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:40,033
one.

54
00:01:40,463 --> 00:01:42,321
So why is he so sure the growth

55
00:01:42,355 --> 00:01:43,377
won't last?

56
00:01:43,433 --> 00:01:44,304
Part two.

57
00:01:44,664 --> 00:01:46,324
The trillion-pound baby.

58
00:01:47,229 --> 00:01:48,750
Marcus has a favorite joke.

59
00:01:49,064 --> 00:01:51,641
A father tweets that his baby doubled its

60
00:01:51,688 --> 00:01:52,547
weight in a month,

61
00:01:52,907 --> 00:01:54,613
and projects that by college,

62
00:01:54,637 --> 00:01:56,773
the kid will weigh a trillion pounds.

63
00:01:57,678 --> 00:01:58,561
Growth like that,

64
00:01:58,607 --> 00:02:01,103
Marcus says, always tails off.

65
00:02:01,463 --> 00:02:03,925
And he thinks Anthropic's already has.

66
00:02:04,273 --> 00:02:05,457
In the second quarter,

67
00:02:05,712 --> 00:02:08,162
revenue grew 147%,

68
00:02:08,185 --> 00:02:10,844
during what he calls the token-maxing fad,

69
00:02:11,076 --> 00:02:13,247
when companies told staff to use as much

70
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:14,745
AI as they could.

71
00:02:15,105 --> 00:02:16,312
In the third quarter,

72
00:02:16,509 --> 00:02:18,495
growth was 47%.

73
00:02:19,273 --> 00:02:20,689
Then there's competition.

74
00:02:20,886 --> 00:02:23,626
Since 2023, he has argued that everyone is

75
00:02:23,696 --> 00:02:25,112
building the same thing,

76
00:02:25,472 --> 00:02:27,341
which means price wars.

77
00:02:27,771 --> 00:02:30,163
Cheaper Chinese models made that real this year,

78
00:02:30,198 --> 00:02:33,472
he says. That leaves Anthropic one niche:

79
00:02:33,831 --> 00:02:35,596
the very top-end models.

80
00:02:35,956 --> 00:02:38,649
Host Alex Kantrowitz put it this way:

81
00:02:39,218 --> 00:02:41,819
if an associate professor can do the job,

82
00:02:42,179 --> 00:02:43,828
why pay the full professor?

83
00:02:44,257 --> 00:02:46,196
And each step up costs more.

84
00:02:46,684 --> 00:02:49,424
One billion to impress users this year,

85
00:02:49,853 --> 00:02:52,431
Marcus warns, ten billion next year,

86
00:02:52,698 --> 00:02:54,439
a hundred billion after that.

87
00:02:54,869 --> 00:02:56,703
Kantrowitz added more pressure:

88
00:02:57,191 --> 00:03:00,326
five customers make up 19% of revenue,

89
00:03:00,593 --> 00:03:02,694
and some big ones are pulling back.

90
00:03:03,472 --> 00:03:07,408
Marcus's conclusion: Anthropic has to stay a rocket.

91
00:03:07,895 --> 00:03:10,867
Every customer who pulls back makes escape velocity

92
00:03:10,948 --> 00:03:12,899
harder. That's the bear case.

93
00:03:12,934 --> 00:03:15,244
Now meet the man who thinks it's wrong.

94
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:16,148
Part three.

95
00:03:16,577 --> 00:03:17,483
The bull case.

96
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:19,898
Aaron Levie runs Box,

97
00:03:20,095 --> 00:03:22,208
which sends huge amounts of business work through

98
00:03:22,254 --> 00:03:23,183
AI models.

99
00:03:23,613 --> 00:03:25,145
He starts with a disclaimer:

100
00:03:25,505 --> 00:03:27,572
he's not giving financial advice,

101
00:03:27,804 --> 00:03:29,232
and he won't say what the shares are

102
00:03:29,267 --> 00:03:29,731
worth.

103
00:03:30,091 --> 00:03:31,554
But on the market itself,

104
00:03:31,693 --> 00:03:32,773
he's very bullish.

105
00:03:33,133 --> 00:03:34,108
His first number:

106
00:03:34,271 --> 00:03:37,963
Anthropic's revenue went from $1.4 billion

107
00:03:38,323 --> 00:03:39,971
in the third quarter of last year

108
00:03:40,331 --> 00:03:43,094
to $17 billion this third quarter.

109
00:03:43,872 --> 00:03:46,403
Twelve times. He doesn't expect that again,

110
00:03:46,485 --> 00:03:48,597
he says, but the direction holds.

111
00:03:48,957 --> 00:03:50,060
His second number:

112
00:03:50,327 --> 00:03:54,112
customers spending at least $100,000 a year

113
00:03:54,681 --> 00:03:58,977
grew from about 950 to about 11,900.

114
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:00,660
Companies, he says,

115
00:04:00,683 --> 00:04:02,076
are voting with their wallets.

116
00:04:02,854 --> 00:04:04,282
Why will it keep going?

117
00:04:04,642 --> 00:04:06,721
Because coding was just the start.

118
00:04:07,394 --> 00:04:09,972
Once AI agents can use computers and write

119
00:04:09,995 --> 00:04:11,133
their own software,

120
00:04:11,492 --> 00:04:14,070
almost every job becomes code-adjacent:

121
00:04:14,848 --> 00:04:17,367
product design, cybersecurity,

122
00:04:17,599 --> 00:04:19,225
research, sales.

123
00:04:19,898 --> 00:04:21,883
For the first time in office work,

124
00:04:21,930 --> 00:04:25,715
Levie says, output isn't limited by how fast

125
00:04:25,785 --> 00:04:26,748
people can type.

126
00:04:27,317 --> 00:04:29,755
The only limit is how much computing power

127
00:04:29,802 --> 00:04:32,251
you buy. And his favorite chart,

128
00:04:32,414 --> 00:04:34,341
from Anthropic's own filing:

129
00:04:35,003 --> 00:04:36,919
in early 2025,

130
00:04:37,035 --> 00:04:39,914
every dollar spent on computing brought back 40

131
00:04:39,984 --> 00:04:41,609
cents. This year,

132
00:04:41,702 --> 00:04:43,316
a dollar seventy-two.

133
00:04:44,082 --> 00:04:45,185
People, he says,

134
00:04:45,382 --> 00:04:46,648
should update their views.

135
00:04:47,008 --> 00:04:48,784
So how does he answer Marcus?

136
00:04:48,933 --> 00:04:49,688
Part four.

137
00:04:50,593 --> 00:04:51,627
Round two.

138
00:04:51,986 --> 00:04:55,075
Kantrowitz put Marcus's biggest worries to Levie.

139
00:04:55,388 --> 00:04:56,236
The debt?

140
00:04:56,909 --> 00:04:59,463
Levie says everything depends on one thing:

141
00:04:59,730 --> 00:05:02,017
whether growth continues at some level.

142
00:05:02,377 --> 00:05:03,852
If the revenue comes,

143
00:05:03,956 --> 00:05:05,350
the commitments are fine.

144
00:05:05,709 --> 00:05:06,568
If it doesn't,

145
00:05:06,638 --> 00:05:07,776
they blow up the company.

146
00:05:08,681 --> 00:05:10,098
Token-maxing?

147
00:05:10,458 --> 00:05:12,675
He calls it a two-week fashion,

148
00:05:12,989 --> 00:05:16,298
not the reason Anthropic's revenue run rate passed

149
00:05:16,344 --> 00:05:17,424
$80 billion.

150
00:05:18,097 --> 00:05:19,363
Diminishing returns?

151
00:05:19,792 --> 00:05:22,393
He says improving a model for one field,

152
00:05:22,753 --> 00:05:24,761
like cybersecurity or law,

153
00:05:24,959 --> 00:05:26,967
costs millions in training data,

154
00:05:27,106 --> 00:05:28,639
not hundreds of billions.

155
00:05:28,999 --> 00:05:31,321
And each of those fields is worth trillions

156
00:05:31,356 --> 00:05:32,401
of dollars of work.

157
00:05:33,178 --> 00:05:35,291
Cheap Chinese and open models?

158
00:05:36,197 --> 00:05:38,055
Levie sees a rising tide.

159
00:05:38,322 --> 00:05:40,934
Companies use a top model to plan and

160
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:42,640
cheap models for the bulk work,

161
00:05:42,954 --> 00:05:44,510
so they spend more on both.

162
00:05:45,288 --> 00:05:47,052
Marcus answers with a warning:

163
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:49,154
more than a dozen studies,

164
00:05:49,177 --> 00:05:51,986
he says, show companies struggling to get a

165
00:05:52,068 --> 00:05:52,788
return,

166
00:05:53,217 --> 00:05:55,911
and hallucinations haven't gone away.

167
00:05:56,689 --> 00:05:58,105
Marcus gave ground too.

168
00:05:58,465 --> 00:06:00,404
He called his doubt about raising the money

169
00:06:00,485 --> 00:06:01,763
his weakest argument,

170
00:06:02,122 --> 00:06:05,211
as reports said the IPO was oversubscribed.

171
00:06:05,698 --> 00:06:08,311
He didn't dispute the tenfold revenue growth.

172
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:11,074
And he agreed that the fallout from Jensen

173
00:06:11,143 --> 00:06:12,061
Huang's scandal

174
00:06:12,490 --> 00:06:15,044
could help Anthropic against Chinese rivals.

175
00:06:15,532 --> 00:06:17,738
"The best argument you've made," he told the

176
00:06:17,784 --> 00:06:18,283
host.

177
00:06:18,957 --> 00:06:19,944
And on one point,

178
00:06:20,141 --> 00:06:21,639
Levie conceded outright.

179
00:06:22,126 --> 00:06:24,286
Could the bills come due before the industries

180
00:06:24,332 --> 00:06:24,948
change?

181
00:06:25,621 --> 00:06:28,454
"Absolutely possible," he said.

182
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:30,137
Part five. Then the market voted.

183
00:06:30,706 --> 00:06:31,937
On November 19th,

184
00:06:31,983 --> 00:06:35,094
Anthropic listed at $83 a share.

185
00:06:35,454 --> 00:06:38,206
It opened around $111 and closed its first

186
00:06:38,241 --> 00:06:39,808
day near $97.

187
00:06:40,238 --> 00:06:43,233
By Friday, it had slipped to about $87,

188
00:06:43,256 --> 00:06:44,812
still above its listing price,

189
00:06:45,172 --> 00:06:46,774
as tech stocks fell around it.

190
00:06:47,204 --> 00:06:48,945
The deal raised more than $80 billion,

191
00:06:49,027 --> 00:06:52,022
and up to $93 billion if the underwriters

192
00:06:52,057 --> 00:06:53,276
take their extra shares.

193
00:06:53,763 --> 00:06:56,004
So far, neither man is proven right.

194
00:06:56,236 --> 00:06:57,003
On the show,

195
00:06:57,049 --> 00:06:59,487
both sides agreed the first day says little.

196
00:06:59,847 --> 00:07:01,565
The real test is time.

197
00:07:01,879 --> 00:07:03,841
Which brings us back to the one number

198
00:07:03,934 --> 00:07:05,838
both of them agree will decide it.

199
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:08,059
Part six. The deciding number.

200
00:07:08,419 --> 00:07:10,347
Strip away the babies and the lemonade,

201
00:07:10,544 --> 00:07:12,390
and both men are making the same bet

202
00:07:12,436 --> 00:07:13,772
in opposite directions.

203
00:07:14,445 --> 00:07:17,336
Levie says everything depends on whether growth continues.

204
00:07:17,765 --> 00:07:20,320
Marcus says Anthropic has to stay a rocket.

205
00:07:20,679 --> 00:07:22,827
So the number to watch is next quarter's

206
00:07:22,874 --> 00:07:26,020
growth. If it bounces back from 47%,

207
00:07:26,043 --> 00:07:27,332
the bull case gets stronger.

208
00:07:27,901 --> 00:07:31,024
If it keeps sliding while those $900 billion

209
00:07:31,094 --> 00:07:32,359
in commitments come due,

210
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:33,903
the bear case does.

211
00:07:34,391 --> 00:07:36,446
And as Ed Zitron argued in our last

212
00:07:36,516 --> 00:07:37,201
episode,

213
00:07:37,688 --> 00:07:40,986
anything under about $21 billion in revenue this

214
00:07:41,055 --> 00:07:42,634
quarter would be a problem.

215
00:07:43,203 --> 00:07:44,840
This is their analysis,

216
00:07:44,933 --> 00:07:46,442
not financial advice.

217
00:07:46,930 --> 00:07:49,368
This was two hours of Big Technology in

218
00:07:49,415 --> 00:07:50,158
eight minutes.

219
00:07:50,518 --> 00:07:52,677
Both full episodes are linked below.

220
00:07:53,107 --> 00:07:54,987
And for the AI money story,

221
